WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.140
- So you sing opera, right?

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:03.363
You were in an opera, right?

00:00:03.363 --> 00:00:06.460
Did, did you actually end up performing?

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.690
- Sometimes I put skis on
and I go down a mountain,

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.470
but that doesn't make me a skier.

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:12.311
You know what I mean?

00:00:12.311 --> 00:00:13.144
So--
- Yes I do.

00:00:13.144 --> 00:00:13.977
That's me in a snowboard.

00:00:13.977 --> 00:00:14.810
(laughing)

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.900
- So I can carry it tune.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:17.733
You know, I had,

00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:19.790
I had a weird stammer when I was a kid

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.400
and I had a music teacher
who helped me with that.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.690
And through music,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.927
I sort of discovered some
things about myself that I,

00:00:26.927 --> 00:00:28.748
that I didn't know.

00:00:28.748 --> 00:00:29.581
- Yeah.

00:00:29.581 --> 00:00:30.414
- And so when I,

00:00:30.414 --> 00:00:31.247
when I decided to try

00:00:31.247 --> 00:00:32.920
and get into the entertainment business,

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.860
the opera represented a loophole.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.200
Like if you can get into the opera,

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.400
you have your AGMA card,

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:40.880
your American Guild of Musical Artists,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.350
and that lets you buy your
Screen Actors Guild card.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.410
What I needed was my
Screen Actors Guild card,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:48.110
but I couldn't get hired
to work in television

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.390
because I wasn't part of the union

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.560
and I couldn't get in the union cause

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.890
I hadn't worked in television.

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.200
- I, it's such a weird thing, I know.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.100
- It's stupid,

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.720
but
(laughing)

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:57.960
the way in for me was

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.080
to learn the shortest aria I could find

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:04.190
and crash an audition
for the Baltimore Opera

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.340
and just sing it as loud as I could

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.670
and hope that they needed
young guys with low voices.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.430
And somehow they let me in

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.110
and I figured I would stay
for a performance or two,

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.600
you know, maybe half a season,

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:19.690
but the music was better than I thought

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:23.701
and the girls were like everywhere.

00:01:23.701 --> 00:01:25.747
(laughing)
- Like everywhere and

00:01:25.747 --> 00:01:27.603
and, y'all are outnumbered so.

00:01:27.603 --> 00:01:29.058
(laughing)

00:01:29.058 --> 00:01:29.891
- I mean.

00:01:29.891 --> 00:01:30.724
- That's awesome.

00:01:30.724 --> 00:01:31.557
I love first of all,

00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:32.960
cause I've sang arias before

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.690
and I grew up singing
operatic stuff all the time.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.260
So I love that you were just like, sure,

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.527
I'll find an aria

00:01:39.527 --> 00:01:42.536
and I'll walk into an
audition and nail it.

00:01:42.536 --> 00:01:44.200
- True story.
- That is bold.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:46.140
- I went to Rosedale Public Library

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.570
and I said to the librarian, I said, look,

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.380
I'm gonna try and force
my way into the opera,

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.830
although that was before Forest Gump.

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.440
I said, I'm gonna fake it you know?

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.610
But give me something
simple that I can memorize.

00:01:57.610 --> 00:01:59.540
So it was a La bohème

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.480
it was the coat aria

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:03.430
and I walked around Baltimore

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:06.930
in like 1984 with a walkman on my head,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.060
listening to Samuel Ramey,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.210
sing it over and over.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:11.540
I didn't know what the words meant.

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.310
I just memorized the
sounds and the melody.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:15.143
- Yeah.

00:02:15.143 --> 00:02:17.380
- And that's all I knew
when I, when I went in.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.400
- Gosh, that's, I mean, that's insane.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.280
That's a gift.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.347
Just, you know, like not everybody--

00:02:21.347 --> 00:02:22.180
- You did the same.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.370
I mean, what was American Idol?

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:24.317
You know.

00:02:24.317 --> 00:02:25.290
- No, but I'm just saying,

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:27.640
but I was trained far before that,

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:29.260
like not in pop singing,

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.320
but I just mean, in

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:31.153
in like singing,

00:02:31.153 --> 00:02:32.100
like I was used to audition.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.840
I was used to like from childhood.

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:34.900
So like that was,

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:35.770
that was a different thing.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:36.603
That was not,

00:02:36.603 --> 00:02:38.710
that was way more bold what you did,

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:40.030
but Mike, I want it.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:41.350
I don't want to put you on the spot,

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:42.183
but I'm gonna,

00:02:42.183 --> 00:02:43.460
like you can say no,

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.290
but I kind of want to hear you sing opera

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.720
'cause your voice has such beautiful tone.

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.420
Can you sing something.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.100
- LJ? You want me to sing something?

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:51.981
- Yeah brother.

00:02:51.981 --> 00:02:52.820
- Yes!

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:54.280
- All right, I'm gonna hit
you with a little Italian.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:55.460
This is the audition.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.070
This is the beginning
of the audition piece.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.500
I had no idea what the words meant.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:02:59.333
I learned them later,

00:02:59.333 --> 00:03:01.875
but this is what got me
into the Baltimore Opera.

00:03:01.875 --> 00:03:02.708
(clearing throat)

00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:03.541
- Okay.

00:03:03.541 --> 00:03:06.624
(singing in Italian)

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.047
- Oh my gosh your tone is magical!

